# Device node types for the two Lenovo-specific character devices.
#
# Names match the stock policy so the labels in file_contexts stay recognisable
# against a stock dump:
#
#   /dev/ttyHS1        the UART the folio keyboard talks over. The keyboard
#                      driver (lenovo_keyboard.ko) is what reports scancodes 654
#                      and 655 for folio open/close, which keylayout/
#                      Vendor_17ef_Product_62b2.kl maps to WAKEUP and SLEEP.
#   /dev/lenovo_penraw raw stylus channel exposed by the Novatek touch driver.
type lenovo_keyboard_device, dev_type;
type lenovo_device, dev_type;

# /dev/afw — Microtrust TEE bridge character device, opened by hal_afw.
type afw_device, dev_type;

# proinfo holds this unit's serials and MAC addresses; prop_init reads the raw
# block device. (Stock's other reader, tndiag, is deliberately not shipped --
# see daemons.te.)
# ⚠️ This comment used to read: "Stock declares this with only the file_type
# attribute, which makes its own blk_file rules unusable. dev_type added here
# deliberately — that is a stock bug, not a convention worth copying."
#
# The first sentence is wrong. An attribute is only needed by rules written
# AGAINST that attribute; a direct `allow SRC vendor_proinfo_block_device:blk_file
# …` needs none, and stock's five such rules (vendor_sepolicy.cil:4306, 4666-4669)
# all work. Nothing about stock's declaration is broken.
#
# `dev_type` is still the right choice, for a reason worth stating properly: it
# is what brings the node under AOSP's ueventd rules (`allow ueventd dev_type:
# blk_file { create setattr … }`, system/sepolicy/private/ueventd.te), so the
# node gets created and labelled with no device-specific rule at all. Stock
# needed an explicit ueventd grant because it did not use dev_type.
#
# The one thing dev_type does NOT bring is setattr for vendor_init — AOSP's only
# dev_type blk_file grant is `getattr` (private/vendor_init.te:219). That is
# deliberate here and is argued in vendor_init.te; do not "fix" it by adding the
# attribute or the rule.
type vendor_proinfo_block_device, dev_type;
